WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.134 --> 00:00:02.435
-Welcome, welcome, welcome to

00:00:02.435 --> 00:00:03.770
"The Tonight Show," everybody!

00:00:03.770 --> 00:00:07.673
[ Cheers and applause ]
What a show.

00:00:07.673 --> 00:00:10.943
You guys, today is officially
the first day of fall.

00:00:10.943 --> 00:00:13.879
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.879 --> 00:00:16.181
Of course, some people are
sad that summer's over,

00:00:16.181 --> 00:00:19.419
but good news -- thanks
to climate change, it's not.

00:00:21.487 --> 00:00:23.790
That's right -- we're entering
that magical time of year where

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.892
we put on old jackets hoping to
find cash in the pockets.

00:00:26.892 --> 00:00:29.395
You know, "Some gum."

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:30.997
Let's get to some news.

00:00:30.997 --> 00:00:32.632 line:0%
After speaking here
in New York City

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:35.167 line:0%
at the UN General Assembly,
President Biden

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:38.304 line:0%
is back in Washington, and
today, he hosted world leaders

00:00:38.304 --> 00:00:41.373 line:0%
for a virtual summit
about COVID.

00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:44.276
Yep, world leaders
traveled 4,000 miles to New York

00:00:44.276 --> 00:00:48.948
just so they could Zoom from
their rooms at the Best Western.

00:00:50.549 --> 00:00:54.487
Well, the summit lasted
more than four hours.

00:00:54.487 --> 00:00:55.955
Four hours!

00:00:55.955 --> 00:00:57.356
By the end,
everyone was fatigued.

00:00:57.356 --> 00:00:59.158
Everyone had body aches,
sore throats.

00:00:59.158 --> 00:01:02.195
They we like, "Wait.
Do we have COVID?"

00:01:02.195 --> 00:01:04.230
But the big takeaway from
the summit is that Biden

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:06.899
wants world leaders
to fully vaccinate 70%

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:08.401
of their population.

00:01:08.401 --> 00:01:12.205
Then world leaders were like,
"Okay, you first.

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:16.976
Deal. You..."

00:01:18.644 --> 00:01:20.913
But this is good -- at the
summit, Biden announced that

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:23.949
the U.S. is buying an additional
500 million doses of

00:01:23.949 --> 00:01:26.953
the Pfizer vaccine to share with
countries around the world.

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:29.656
Each country
will receive a basket with

00:01:29.656 --> 00:01:32.492
an injectable arrangement,
which is interesting.

00:01:34.394 --> 00:01:36.328
That's right --
The White House says

00:01:36.328 --> 00:01:39.665
they're gonna to start
sending the doses in January.

00:01:39.665 --> 00:01:41.567
Seriously, January?

00:01:41.567 --> 00:01:43.936
Countries are like, "If I sign
up for Government Prime,

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:45.838
can I get that tomorrow?

00:01:45.838 --> 00:01:47.574
January? Wow."

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:48.641
This is amazing.

00:01:48.641 --> 00:01:50.076 line:0%
Last night,
the winning ticket for

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:53.646 line:0%
the $432 million
Mega Millions jackpot

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:57.217 line:0%
was sold a block away from our
studio, at Pronto Pizza.

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:58.251 line:0%
-Ooh!

00:01:58.251 --> 00:01:59.686
-Dang!

00:01:59.686 --> 00:02:01.854
-When asked what they'll do with
$432 million, the winner said,

00:02:01.854 --> 00:02:03.055
"Hey, it's New York City.

00:02:03.055 --> 00:02:05.057
I'm gonna move from my studio
to a one-bedroom.

00:02:05.057 --> 00:02:06.759
Come on.

00:02:06.759 --> 00:02:08.861
Yeah."

00:02:08.861 --> 00:02:11.397
Pronto Pizza.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:13.466 line:0%
And, finally, I saw
that Aston Martin is selling

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:18.871 line:0%
a James Bond car for kids,
and it costs $123,000.

00:02:18.871 --> 00:02:20.873
The kid who gets one
is going to be like,

00:02:20.873 --> 00:02:22.008
"I'm a brat.

00:02:22.008 --> 00:02:23.843
Spoiled brat."

00:02:23.843 --> 00:02:25.011
Welcome to the show.

00:02:25.011 --> 00:02:27.012
Just a reminder
to tune in tomorrow night.

00:02:27.012 --> 00:02:30.182
We're partnering with Samsung
to do a very special show

00:02:30.182 --> 00:02:32.819
from all five boroughs of
New York City --

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:37.056
Manhattan, Queens, Brooklyn,
Staten Island, and the Bronx.

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:38.390
There's lots of surprises.

00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:40.593
The Killers are going to perform
at the Top of The Rock.

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:42.629
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:44.097 --> 00:02:45.798
And you'll get to
see me throw out

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:47.900
the first pitch at
a Yankees game.

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:50.003
-Ooh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.737
-Ooh!

00:02:51.737 --> 00:02:53.205
-It's tomorrow night.
-Tomorrow night.

00:02:53.205 --> 00:02:54.707
-You don't want to miss it,
but if you happen to miss it...

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:55.708
-Yeah, it's okay.

00:02:55.708 --> 00:02:57.109
-Dude, I got to be
honest, man --

00:02:57.109 --> 00:02:58.678
throwing out the first pitch,
this is a lot of pressure.

00:02:58.678 --> 00:02:59.946
I never wanted to do this thing.

00:02:59.946 --> 00:03:01.614
And now I apologize to Questlove

00:03:01.614 --> 00:03:03.048
because I've made fun of
his first pitch.

00:03:03.048 --> 00:03:04.617
-You did a lot.

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:05.985
-No, not really.
I just played it.

00:03:05.985 --> 00:03:07.119
-You just played it a little.

00:03:07.119 --> 00:03:09.288
-I just played it,
and the audience laughed.

00:03:09.288 --> 00:03:11.557
I laughed with it.
-Not at him.

00:03:11.557 --> 00:03:13.326
-Not at him.
I laughed with the video.

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:14.994
-What you did --
you took Kamal's advice.

00:03:14.994 --> 00:03:18.731
Kamal said, "Just throw it
into the stands."

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:21.167
-No, I didn't throw it
into the stands.

00:03:21.167 --> 00:03:23.669
-Yeah, obviously.
-Well, the catcher caught it.

00:03:23.669 --> 00:03:24.737
-Yeah.
-So, yeah.

00:03:24.737 --> 00:03:26.839
-Was he at home base
when he caught it

00:03:26.839 --> 00:03:27.907
or did he --

00:03:27.907 --> 00:03:29.842
-No, he was definitely
at home base.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:30.910
He might have even
jumped up a little.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:31.910
-Yeah, he jumped.

00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:33.546
-So, the most --
-Here's the thing.

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:34.713
-I think the
most important thing was

00:03:34.713 --> 00:03:36.448
doing better than Quest, right?

00:03:36.448 --> 00:03:37.817
-Yeah, that was the whole goal.
-Right.

00:03:37.817 --> 00:03:39.519
-Yeah, so, I did that.
But it was definitely --

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:41.387
[ Laughter ]

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:42.387
-Did you?

00:03:42.387 --> 00:03:43.389
-It's definitely
a lot of pressure.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:44.924
Did you throw from the mound

00:03:44.924 --> 00:03:46.793
or you throw from
in front of the mound?

00:03:46.793 --> 00:03:48.962
-I threw it from the mound.
-I was on the mound.

00:03:48.962 --> 00:03:50.029
-Wow.

00:03:50.029 --> 00:03:51.564
-I went all the way up
to the mound.

00:03:51.564 --> 00:03:53.633
-Yeah.

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.601
-I didn't have
any rehearsal at all.

00:03:55.601 --> 00:03:57.403
-No rehearsal with the sports.
No.

00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:59.339
-No, I mean...

00:04:00.573 --> 00:04:02.474
-That's great.

00:04:02.474 --> 00:04:04.377
-What I --
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:04.377 --> 00:04:05.878
You start shaking.

00:04:05.878 --> 00:04:08.414
I was like -- Mariano Rivera,
who's amazing,

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:10.015
the greatest pitcher
of all time --

00:04:10.015 --> 00:04:11.150
greatest relief pitcher --

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:13.419
was there,
and he kind of helped me out.

00:04:13.419 --> 00:04:15.187
You'll see it tomorrow.
And he was a great guy.

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.256
I'll talk more about
Mariano on Friday.

00:04:17.256 --> 00:04:19.892
But I kept thinking about Quest,
because it's a lot --

00:04:19.892 --> 00:04:21.593
I started just kind of shaking,

00:04:21.593 --> 00:04:23.029
because I had never been
that nervous in a while.

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:25.331
And there wasn't that many
people in the stands still yet,

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:28.334
and so I was kind of happy
that not that many --

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:29.434
But then I forgot
that it's broadcast

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:31.336
to millions of people.

00:04:31.336 --> 00:04:32.939
And, so, I get up there
and I go,

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.973
"Alright, so, don't --
don't hit the dirt."

00:04:34.973 --> 00:04:36.042
That's the one thing.

00:04:36.042 --> 00:04:37.443
And when you throw out
a first pitch,

00:04:37.443 --> 00:04:39.012
people that know
you're throwing it out

00:04:39.012 --> 00:04:41.447
start sending you
clips of bad pitches,

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:42.915
and they go, "Don't do this.

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.550
Don't do this one
or don't do that one."

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:47.319
I go -- And I got 25 clips of

00:04:47.319 --> 00:04:48.955
Questlove throwing the ball,
and I go --

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:50.189
-From different angles?

00:04:50.189 --> 00:04:51.991
-I'm joking. I'm joking.
Different? No. I'm joking.

00:04:51.991 --> 00:04:53.426
-You were laughing
with him again.

00:04:53.426 --> 00:04:57.263
-Yeah. So, I get -- So, anyways,
I do it and I go just --

00:04:57.263 --> 00:04:58.765
And then I go up to the mound,

00:04:58.765 --> 00:05:01.600
and it seems further away
than possible.

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:04.537
Like, when was the last time you
threw anything 60 feet?

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:05.571
-Never.

00:05:05.571 --> 00:05:07.272
-Well...

00:05:07.272 --> 00:05:08.573
-I can't even walk 60 feet

00:05:08.573 --> 00:05:10.109
without running out of --

00:05:10.109 --> 00:05:11.978
-Yeah, I had to throw
a ball 60 feet.

00:05:11.978 --> 00:05:13.046
And I'm not an athlete.

00:05:13.046 --> 00:05:14.480
These guys are
professional athletes.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.783
A lot of credit to
the baseball players.

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:21.087
But I threw it and I was just
kind of nervous even doing that.

00:05:21.087 --> 00:05:24.923
I even, like -- When I went
back, I kind of rocked into it.

00:05:24.923 --> 00:05:26.659
-Mm-hmm. That's good.

00:05:26.659 --> 00:05:27.960
-Like, I've never --
I've never seen anyone do --

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:29.161
-Wait. Do it again. Do it again.

00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:30.163
-I did like a --
-Like The Temptations?

00:05:30.163 --> 00:05:32.598
-♪ Sugar pie, honeybunch ♪

00:05:32.598 --> 00:05:35.067
♪ You know that I love you ♪

00:05:35.067 --> 00:05:36.569
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:37.904
♪ I can't help my-- ♪

00:05:37.904 --> 00:05:41.274
I was doing --
You know, so, I rocked --

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:44.076
I rocked into it
too much to kick it out.

00:05:44.076 --> 00:05:45.077
-Right.

00:05:45.077 --> 00:05:46.846
-So, what I was doing --

00:05:46.846 --> 00:05:48.881
I was choreographing,
way overthinking.

00:05:48.881 --> 00:05:50.917
I'm like...
♪♪

00:05:53.285 --> 00:05:54.987
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:54.987 --> 00:05:57.056
And I was doing --
It was terrible.

00:05:57.056 --> 00:05:59.057
And then I released it.

00:05:59.057 --> 00:06:00.560
Oh, I kicked --
-What?!

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:03.095
-I release, follow through.

00:06:03.095 --> 00:06:04.363
-Right.

00:06:04.363 --> 00:06:08.434
-And it was almost like
a combination of, like,

00:06:08.434 --> 00:06:10.703
Bret Saberhagen...
-Right.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:13.873
-...and Dr. Fauci.

00:06:13.873 --> 00:06:15.474
It was one of those two.
-Yeah.

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:17.542
-It didn't quite work,
but it did work.

00:06:17.542 --> 00:06:21.513
And then I just kind of said,
"Yeah, alright, good."

00:06:21.513 --> 00:06:22.815
And I just said, "Okay."

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:24.483
And I just --
I didn't even text Quest,

00:06:24.483 --> 00:06:26.419
because I just go,
"Hopefully he didn't see it."

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:28.354
-I was watching you.
-I know you did.

00:06:28.354 --> 00:06:30.189
But you'll see it tomorrow.

00:06:30.189 --> 00:06:31.757
And, again, thank you
to the Yankees.

00:06:31.757 --> 00:06:33.325
It's an honor to do it.
-Yeah.

00:06:33.325 --> 00:06:35.761
-So much pressure.
I was like, "Oh, my gosh!"

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:37.329
Quest, I apologize.

00:06:37.329 --> 00:06:40.066
It's a tough thing to do.
[ Applause ]

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.103
You'll see it tomorrow night
on our show

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:46.405
and you'll see exactly
what went down.

00:06:46.405 --> 00:06:48.207
But let's talk about
our show tonight.

00:06:48.207 --> 00:06:50.542
They star in the new Hulu series

00:06:50.542 --> 00:06:52.945
"Only Murders in the Building."

00:06:52.945 --> 00:06:54.313
Steve Martin
and Martin Short are here.

00:06:54.313 --> 00:06:56.515
-Oh, come on!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:56.515 --> 00:06:59.284
In studio.
-"Only Murders in the Building."

00:06:59.284 --> 00:07:01.887
-So great.

00:07:01.887 --> 00:07:04.890
-Plus, you can see her in
the new limited series "Maid,"

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.292
premiering October 1st
on Netflix.

00:07:07.292 --> 00:07:09.762
Margaret Qualley is here!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:09.762 --> 00:07:12.497
My partner, my pal.

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:15.401
And we have an in-studio
performance from Cynthia Erivo!

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:16.402
-Oh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:16.402 --> 00:07:18.737
-Look at that.

00:07:18.737 --> 00:07:21.707
Gorgeous, sounds great.

00:07:21.707 --> 00:07:22.909
Get ready. What a show tonight.

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:24.644
Also, this is
a special night to be here,

00:07:24.644 --> 00:07:26.545
because it is Climate Night.

00:07:26.545 --> 00:07:31.016
Tonight, we're joining up with
all the other late-night shows

00:07:31.016 --> 00:07:32.952
to raise awareness
about climate change.

00:07:32.952 --> 00:07:36.121
And tonight, we have our special
guest Dr. Jane Goodall on.

00:07:36.121 --> 00:07:37.656
-Oh!
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:37.656 --> 00:07:39.858
Come on.
-My favorite.

00:07:39.858 --> 00:07:41.226
I love her.
-Come on.

00:07:41.226 --> 00:07:42.461
-She is unbelievable.

00:07:42.461 --> 00:07:43.762
She's going to be on the show
to talk about

00:07:43.762 --> 00:07:45.897
a new campaign, Trees for Jane.

00:07:45.897 --> 00:07:47.065 line:0%
If you want to learn more about

00:07:47.065 --> 00:07:50.069 line:0%
how to help,
go to the climategroup.org,

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:51.204 line:0%
and you can follow along tonight

00:07:51.204 --> 00:07:53.206 line:0%
by using the hashtag
"ClimateNight."

00:07:53.206 --> 00:07:55.241 line:0%
It's a cool thing, and we're
very happy to be a part of it.

00:07:55.241 --> 00:07:57.243 line:0%
Thanks.
[ Cheers and applause ]

